analysis:
Reviewed three files in this batch.

1) PromptPreparationBenchmark.swift is a new CPU-only benchmark in ProviderBenchmark. It builds synthetic conversations from a deterministic PRNG, with no real prompts and no network or secrets. Prompt-cache scopes are random UUIDs generated per run. It writes no files and only returns a JSON report string, so I found no data-exposure path. It depends on PromptPreparationProbe, which is not in the supplied evidence, so its behavior is unverified.

2) EngineV2Factory+BenchmarkPrompt.swift (head lines 20-29) replaces three separate body parses (decodeOpenAIRequest, extractChatTemplateControls, extractSamplingOverrides, extractLogprobsSpec) with a single ProviderLoop.decodeInboundChatRequest. This is an SPI-only benchmarking helper. It keeps the media, stop, response_format and tool-choice guards unchanged. I could not see decodeInboundChatRequest, so equivalence of the extracted extension fields is unverified. The risk is limited to benchmark fidelity.

3) MultiModelBatchSchedulerEngine.swift (head ~lines 795-797) passes a new promptCacheScope into ProviderPromptContractPipeline.tokenize. The scope is `cacheEnabled ? requestCacheScope : nil`, which is the same scope that partitions the prefix KV cache (T-041). The value comes from the coordinator-authored cacheScope, or from nativeLocalCacheScope for diffusion, so callers cannot choose it. When cacheEnabled is false, which covers legacy or malformed coordinators, no token reuse happens. This mirrors the KV cache isolation. The remaining integration question is the new token-reuse cache inside the pipeline, which is outside the supplied evidence. It should be checked for:
- keying by the scope in full, with no collision on an empty scope, since the local path gives cacheScope "" with cacheEnabled true;
- bounded memory;
- no persistence to disk;
- no cross-scope sharing, which would add a TTFT or timing oracle beyond the accepted SEC-035 risk and would retain plaintext prompt-derived tokens in memory (A-001).

I found no concrete regression in the supplied code. Because the cache implementation is unseen, the T-041 conclusion is not fully established.
